well the nato meeting this week was a  show of western unity and strength in  the face of russian aggression but  divisions of course do remain between  the uk and the eu closer to home  specifically over the northern ireland  protocol and the british government's  plans to change or scrap parts of it the  eu says it will respond with legal  action some think it could lead to a  trade war so what is the state of the  relationship between the uk and the eu's  largest and most powerful nation  germany's ambassador to the uk mcgraw  berger joins us now thank you very much  for being  with us um i just want to start with  ukraine after that interview with lord  danette who said that the perhaps the  most likely scenario is the war goes  into deep freeze russia takes the  donbass and it's very difficult then to  get those russian troops out is that  also germany's assessment  yeah i think it's one of the possible  outcomes of this terrible war that we  might go into a deep freeze but you know  i would not exclude that russia might  push towards odessa because we have so  far not seen that putin has scaled down  on his ambitions  and putin is watching very closely and  the the unity of of the western alliance  but you know i think the messages which  came out of the g7 summit especially the  nato summit  with the 300 000 troops now on high  alert i think that at least has sent a  very strong message towards russia  given the russian aggression that you  speak of there  do you feel that germany has been a bit  sluggish in  providing heavy weapons to ukraine  particularly in comparison to the uk and  the us  no i i would say that the difference was  that in germany we had really to change  totally  our approach and that was made by  chancellor schultz in his speech very  early in february on site and vend which  means really a total new approach  concerning 100 billion for the german  army which means that we are going to  have the largest armed forces in europe  again we have started later than the us  and uk sending heavy weapons but i think  the multi  rocket launchers which we are sending  together with us and uk  but also the hovitzer self-propelled  hobbitses i think they are already  having an effect we always can and we  have to do more  and  sophie my concern is really that we as a  as nato as western alliance we have to  be capable of supporting ukraine if this  war drags on for weeks and months and  that will be also a challenge for our  armed forces  i understand of course the shift in  german position but it has taken a while  to get to this point this is president  zielenski just two weeks ago every  leader of our partner countries and  naturally the chancellor schultz of  germany knows exactly what ukraine needs  it just that the deliveries from germany  are still less than they could be it has  taken a long time for you to get here  you will acknowledge that  yes as i said we started later than  other countries with the delivery but  you know we have done other issues for  example we did  exchanges of arms with the czech  republic allowing them to deliver  russian-made tanks to ukraine we did the  same with greece we sent our  missile defense systems to slovakia  allowing them  to send systems that is something that  other countries have not done so if you  take the overall picture i think germany  is very close to what the uk is doing  and of course germany is very reliant on  russia for oil and gas do you think you  rely on russia for more than a third of  its gas and the german foreign minister  has said that there's a hybrid war going  on as russia cuts those supplies to  europe how concerned are you about the  supply of oil and gas  yes i think we have to be  very concerned especially on gas on oil  we have managed to reduce in the last  two three months the dependency from  over 30 percent to only 12 percent  because you have an international oil  market where you can get oil from other  countries on gas it's not so easy you  need infrastructure  you need lng terminals we have invested  in them  so we have bought four floating lng  terminals which will arrive in germany  before the end of the year  but obviously if russia and russia has  reduced gas delivery to only 40 percent  so germany has declared gas emergency  and that means if this continues or if  russia would really cut gas supply  that we will have to  stop delivery to some  sectors of our industry that heating in  the autumn and winter might turn into a  problem so i can tell you there is quite  some concern  um okay i want to talk to you a little  bit now about the northern ireland uh  protocol if i may you're in belfast at  last week  do you accept that the northern ireland  protocol currently isn't working and are  you optimistic that there is a solution  that can be found  yeah you know for me it was very  interesting these three days in belfast  talking with with all stakeholders  and the impression i got especially from  the business community is yes there are  problems especially for  small retailers and we think they need  and they can be addressed  the majority of business told us that  they are working very good with the  northern ireland protocol what they need  is certainty  certainty in order to attract investment  and this certainty is definitely damaged  by the northern ireland protocol bill  and i can tell you that we are very  concerned  that this bill which is not only  um from the point of view of  international law it it it would really  um break an agreement which the united  kingdom has signed and ratified only two  years ago but more than that it brings  uncertainty to northern ireland next  year we're going to have 25 years of the  good friday belfast agreement and i  think it should be our joint endeavor to  do whatever is possible to have a  functioning executive in northern  ireland um an assembly and a business  community which can move forward so just  to be clear because you mentioned  international law that you believe that  the northern ireland protocol bill  breaks international law that it's  effectively illegal  there is no doubt about it and i think  theresa may has said all about this  position we share it  this is breaking an agreement and i  think that  lawmakers house of commons they should  be very concerned about what it means to  break an international agreement how  that will be seen at the international  stage so how can we see how how will it  be seen what will the reaction  you know it will obviously be seen as a  challenge to international law and to  rule of law in general  and so i think the best what can be done  is to go back into legality and that  means  let's negotiate find technical and  practical solutions on the basis of the  protocol the european union is ready to  do that but what we also cannot accept  is a challenge and a risk to our  internal market so what would happen if  the uk government does press ahead with  it are we talking about a trade war you  know i i  only can tell you at that stage that all  options are on the table  and i would not exclude any one of that  but really our call is let's go back  into negotiations this is doable it's  solvable and it's in the best interest  of northern ireland okay and just very  very finally i'm going to squeeze one  more question in um  looking ahead we have the world cup in  qatar if england win their group and all  the other rounds as well and germany do  the same  they'll meet in the final  bearing in mind that you're a diplomat  who do you think would win that one  that will be a fantastic game because  always england against germany in the  world cups was  were great games so i'm really looking  forward to that but now first of all we  are looking forward to the women's  european championship in england  and there we also have a very strong  women's team competing  well that is a textbook diplomats answer  i have to say thank you very much for  being on the program this morning thank  you thank you for having me
 

